      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">By: Todd Estes</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Thanks to everyone who has made this year of change a good one for the department.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I take a great deal of pride in my colleagues and their work, and this newsletter shows that we all have much of which we can be proud. I won't rehearse it all in this introduction—please read for yourself below. Key to all of the success we experience are the talented people who work with us in the department. I'd like to say a word of thanks to <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span> who is concluding two years as our graduate assistant. One professor told me that although we have had many great graduate assistants, this is the best one ever. She has done excellent work for us and for our students and we acknowledge her contributions and thank her for her efforts. I also want to thank <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span> who concluded his first year</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">THREE HISTORIANS RECEIVE $10,000 SUMMER RESEARCH FELLOWSHIPS</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Three History department faculty members have won $10,000 Summer Research Fellowships for the summer of 2015 to fund their research and scholarship. These fellowships are administered by the University Research Committee and announced last December, with Elizabeth Shesko, Erin Dwyer, and Todd Estes being the recipients. The fellowships are for projects titled: "Negotiating Autonomy in the Bolivian Barracks, 1906-1996," "The Length of Their Shadows: The Lives of Women in the Early American Republic," and "The Politics of the Early American Republic."</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">These results continue the department's strong record of winning research grants. With three faculty members receiving these fellowships, the department has demonstrated its commitment to supporting faculty scholarship. It is especially gratifying to note that two of the recipients, Erin Dwyer and Liz Shesko, have been working on projects that are deeply rooted in the history of the region and are of great interest to the department. The department has made great recent strides in the area of graduate education, and this funding success reflects well on us all.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Congratulations to all three recipients and to the department as a whole for this latest round of funding success that reflects well on us all.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">One of the highlights of each year is the department's participation in the PAT Regional Conference, which was held in March at the University of Tennessee. Our students presented papers, and two others made the trip to provide support. Four of the presenters took home awards for their excellent work. The department also had the pleasure of hosting <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>, who won an award for "Best Paper in their categories," and <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>, who won an award for "Most Outstanding Student." Congratulations to the award winners and also to all of our presenters and attendees who performed very impressively, according to faculty sponsors Dan Clark and James Naus. Dan and James both noted the impressive college and professional our students were and how well they represented the department and the university. Also presenting for the department was <span data-field="full_name">{{ full_name }}</span>, who is a rising star in the department. I am thrilled to have her as a colleague.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The department is proud to support the continued success of our students and faculty. We are committed to providing opportunities for growth and discovery in the field of history. We encourage all students to participate in departmental events and to seek out opportunities to learn from our faculty and peers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

