    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">INTERVIEW WITH ERIN DWYER</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">We also sat down with Erin Dwyer, one of our faculty members, to talk about her life and work.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: How have you enjoyed your first year at Oakland and in Michigan?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">It has certainly been an action-packed year! After a great Fall semester, I'm excited to try out a new role. I've been enjoying the chance to teach and to get to know the students. The Oakland community, students, faculty, and staff, were all incredibly welcoming. I'm looking forward to the spring semester and to also work with the department on new initiatives. I'm really feeling in love with Detroit and all its offer.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: Have there been any particular highlights of your first year at Oakland and in Michigan?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I was thrilled to receive the College of Arts and Sciences Engagement Award, which has enabled me to do so much more to catalog the Krege Archives. I've been working with the department to digitize and catalog the archives, and the included, and received Gordon Miller's support in this endeavor. I'm also grateful for the opportunity to work with the department on new initiatives.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">Q: What do you hope will be the impact of your work on the field?</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I see myself as a part of a new trend in history, one that is moving away from the grand narratives and toward the stories of individuals and communities. I hope my work can contribute to this shift, by bringing more nuance and complexity to the historical record. I am also interested in how history can be used to foster dialogue about contemporary issues, such as immigration and social justice.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: What kinds of news classes do you enjoy teaching here at Oakland?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I'm already teaching the IS Introduction to Latin America, a course that allows students to engage with the region and the 360-level courses that build on that foundation. I'm also excited to teach other 300-level courses to the students, particularly those focused on indigenous movements in Latin America.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">INTERVIEW WITH DR. DYER</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">We also sat down with Erin Dwyer, one of our faculty members, to talk about her life and work.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: How have you enjoyed your first year at Oakland and in Michigan?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">It has certainly been an action-packed year! After a great Fall semester, I'm excited to try out a new role. I've been enjoying the chance to teach and to get to know the students. The Oakland community, students, faculty, and staff, were all incredibly welcoming. I'm looking forward to the spring semester and to also work with the department on new initiatives. I'm really feeling in love with Detroit and all its offer.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: Have there been any particular highlights of your first year at Oakland and in Michigan?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I was thrilled to receive the College of Arts and Sciences Engagement Award, which has enabled me to do so much more to catalog the Krege Archives. I've been working with the department to digitize and catalog the archives, and the included, and received Gordon Miller's support in this endeavor. I'm also grateful for the opportunity to work with the department on new initiatives.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 40px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">Q: What do you believe will be the most important aspect of your work on the field?</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I believe that the most important aspect of my work is to challenge the established narratives and to give voice to the marginalized. History is not just about the great men and the big events; it is about the lives of ordinary people and the forces that shape their experiences. I want to uncover those stories and present them in a way that is accessible and engaging to a broad audience.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Q: What are some of your favorite historical topics?</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">I am fascinated by the history of migration, the impact of global trade on local communities, and the ways in which cultural exchange has shaped societies. I am particularly interested in the intersection of environmental history and social history.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt;">REFLECTIONS BY GEORGE MILNE</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The department's newest tenured faculty member, George Milne, shares some perspectives on his work, his career, and his views on the department.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">During the 2014-15 academic year, I was able to pass two career milestones. The first was the publication of my book, <span data-field="book_title">{{ book_title }}</span>. It is a study of the complex relationship between race and land in the region. The second was the appointment to the <span data-field="department_name">{{ department_name }}</span> faculty. I am grateful for the support of the department and my colleagues.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 11pt;">My research has focused on the history of the American South, particularly the dynamics of race and labor. I have also been interested in the history of the Caribbean and Latin America. I hope to continue to explore these areas and to contribute to the ongoing scholarly conversation in the field of history.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

